          "context": "## What was asked\n\nA shorter README that keeps the main points of CHAP, its goals, the links\nand the details people need, with the rest moved to other documents, and\nto ABOUT.md where it belongs there. You then asked for the concepts to stay\nas descriptive as they were, and for the 90-second tour to go to a page of\nits own. It goes out with 0.3.4.\n\n## What changed, and why\n\n- **README.md** keeps the header and badges, the before and after picture,\n  what CHAP is for and its four goals, the concepts as they were, one line\n  each to install it, the override envelope's picture with a short\n  explanation, a short status, the reading table and the citation. It goes\n  from about four hundred lines to about a hundred and fifty.\n- **docs/tour.md** is new: the 90-second tour's clip, the code for each\n  step in TypeScript and Python, and the override analyser's report.\n- **ABOUT.md** gains what the override envelope's fields point at, and\n  reading the log back with chap-analytics, and its contents, reading paths\n  and layout list the tour's page.\n- **START_HERE.md** lists the libraries, the templates and the profile\n  explorer among its ways in.\n- **IN_PRACTICE.md** links the tour's page where it pointed at the README's\n  tour.\n- **CHANGELOG.md** gains a Documentation line in the 0.3.4 entry.\n\n## How it was checked\n\n- Every Markdown link in the repository resolves, and so do the README's\n  HTML links and images.\n- The anchors other documents use to point into the README and ABOUT.md\n  are still there.\n- The em-dash and version checks pass. No code changes.\n\n## Look closely at\n\n- The README from top to bottom, as a first-time visitor reads it.\n- docs/tour.md, which should read on its own."
